WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:03.070
(bright music)

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.610
- [Narrator] Have you ever
walked out of a building

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.185
on a sunny day and had an
uncontrollable urge to sneeze?

00:00:08.185 --> 00:00:09.630
- Achoo.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.400
- [Narrator] If you answered
yes, like me, you likely suffer

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.790
from a strange genetic
condition that has remained

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.433
unexplained by medical
science for millennia.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.230
And if you answered no
you probably have no idea

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.380
what I'm talking about.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.570
Sun sneezing, as it's more commonly known,

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:30.160
is a medical condition
called photic sneeze reflex.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.280
- [Louis] Another name
that some have given is ...

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:33.830
- [Narrator] Hold on.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:37.260
Before we get into all that,
this is Dr. Louis Ptacek,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.230
a neurologist and a human geneticist

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.170
at the University of
California, San Francisco.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:43.030
OK, go ahead.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.860
- [Louis] Another name
that some have given

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:49.660
is autosomal dominant compelling
helio-ophthalmic outburst.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.570
Or ACHOO syndrome.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.890
But P.S.R. is really the appropriate name

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:54.930
that we should all use.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.970
Photic sneeze reflex
is a tendency to sneeze

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.210
when suddenly exposed to bright light.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.507
- Achoo.

00:01:01.507 --> 00:01:03.590
- [Narrator] And as
strange as that may sound,

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.490
it's more common than you might think.

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:09.457
- [Louis] I estimate that
about one in 10 or 10 percent

00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:11.641
of all people have this condition.

00:01:11.641 --> 00:01:12.474
- Achoo.

00:01:12.474 --> 00:01:14.070
- [Narrator] So a lot of us are affected.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.520
But how and why it happens
is not fully understood.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.930
Despite the fact that humans
have been pondering it

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.810
for thousands of years.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.680
In 350 BC, Aristotle posed the question,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.840
"Why does the heat of the
sun provoke sneezing?"

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.970
Aristotle was barking up
the wrong tree, though.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.290
- [Louis] This doesn't
make sense because heat

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:38.030
in and of itself doesn't cause sneezing.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.100
- [Narrator] Some 2,000
years later, the philosopher

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.630
Sir Francis Bacon also
tried to figure it out.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.520
His theory was that light from the sun

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.690
caused your eyes to water, which
then seeped into your nose,

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:49.555
triggering a sneeze.

00:01:49.555 --> 00:01:50.388
- Achoo.

00:01:50.388 --> 00:01:51.885
- [Louis] It's an interesting thought,

00:01:51.885 --> 00:01:52.718
but it doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:01:52.718 --> 00:01:55.220
P.S.R. occurs almost instantaneously.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.610
And it takes longer for
the tear ducts to react.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.842
Furthermore, tears don't cause sneezing.

00:02:01.842 --> 00:02:04.580
There's no evidence for that.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.690
- [Narrator] So modern medicine
has come up with its own

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:10.550
theories, and it has to do
with the wiring in your head.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.320
- [Louis] Sneezes are triggered typically

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.630
by irritating things in our nose.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.640
And these are detected by a
nerve-the trigeminal nerve.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.205
- [Narrator] And right next
door to the trigeminal nerve

00:02:21.205 --> 00:02:22.038
is the optic nerve.

00:02:22.038 --> 00:02:22.950
You may have heard of this one.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.320
- [Louis] The optic nerve is
stimulated by bright light

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.790
and sends electric signals to the brain.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:30.987
Some of that signal can be
picked up by the trigeminal nerve

00:02:30.987 --> 00:02:33.160
and misinterpreted by the brain

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.533
as an irritation in the nose.

00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:38.090
- [Narrator] Which triggers,
you guessed it, a sneeze.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.260
Well, that's the theory anyway.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.910
- [Louis] Photic sneeze
reflex is not a disease.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.960
Consequently, the medical
community hasn't paid a whole lot

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.370
of attention to it.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.270
Therefore, we know very
little about what causes it.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.290
- [Narrator] But Dr. Ptacek
believes that understanding

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.660
P.S.R. could potentially shed some light

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.830
on more serious conditions.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:57.850
- [Louis] Epileptic seizures

00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.670
can be caused by strobing lights.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.780
Migraine headache is
precipitated by light.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.450
If we can understand what the mechanism

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.140
is that leads to P.S.R.
this may give insights into

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:12.090
other conditions, which
actually are medically important

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.820
and represent disorders.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:15.700
- [Narrator] So, there you have it.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.620
This 2,000-year-old medical mystery

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.543
may be worth solving after all.

